 {:seon.task/name "a-schema-transaction-costs-the-attributes-it-declares", :seon.task/kind :work, :seon.task/plan "What exists (measured 2026-11-13, deps, built program, a store of Seon's schema): a transaction declaring n attributes costs about 5.7M ticks fixed plus ~33k an attribute (10 attrs 6.2M, 30 6.9M, 100 9.1M, 300 15.7M); the first in a fresh process ~30M for 100. A data transaction of ~10 datoms costs ~0.1M (docs/cost.md#what-things-cost-here). The fixed part is ~60x a datom's and does not grow with n, so it is per transaction: the likely cause is the schema index (rschema, ident maps) or Seon's write check rebuilt over every attribute the store declares on each schema change, not over the ones that changed (AGENTS.md §3a.2). Find which (split one schema transaction's ticks: the transaction's own work, the schema index rebuild, the check), and make it cost the attributes it declares: estimate ~40k an attribute, ~0.2M fixed. Seen in seon.merge-cost-test/a-merge-reads-the-data-attributes-from-the-index (its three fixtures declare 140 attributes in three transactions: ~45M of its ~66M). See `docs/storage.md#the-write-checks-every-entity-it-touches`.", :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-schema-transaction-costs-the-attributes-it-declares}}]
